.TH IAD\-DATE\-INPUT 3 "" "iced-adaptive-design 0.1.0" "iced-adaptive-design Manual"
.SH NAME
date_input \- a date entry with a calendar popover
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
DateInput::new(text: &'a str, view: Date, on_input: impl Fn(String) \-> M + 'a)
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBDateInput\fR: a date entry with a calendar popover.
.PP
The text is typed as \fBYYYY\-MM\-DD\fR (parsed by \fBDate::parse\fR); the calendar button opens a month grid to pick a day. Everything is controlled: you keep the text, the month on show and the chosen date, and the widget reports \fBon_input(text)\fR, \fBon_pick(date)\fR and \fBon_view(first\-of\-month)\fR. Text that does not parse, or is outside \fBmin\fR/\fBmax\fR, draws the entry in its error state. Date arithmetic is dependency\-free (\fBDate\fR).
.PP
.nf
.RS
DateInput::new(&self.text, self.view, Message::Text)
    .selected(self.date)
    .on_pick(Message::Picked)
    .on_view(Message::View)
.RE
.fi
.PP
The calendar stays open while you page through months or pick a day (click outside or press Escape to close it).
.SH CONSTRUCTORS
.TP
.B DateInput::new(text: &'a str, view: Date, on_input: impl Fn(String) \-> M + 'a)
\fBtext\fR is the entry's content, \fBview\fR any date in the month the calendar shows.
.SH PROPERTIES
Builder methods, chained after the constructor.
.TP
.B \&.selected(d: Option<Date>)
The chosen date, highlighted in the calendar.
.TP
.B \&.level(l: Level)
The level on show (default \fBMonth\fR); the \fBview\fR date of \fBnew\fR picks the period. Clamped to \fBmin_level\fR..=\fBmax_level\fR.
.TP
.B \&.calendar(v: CalendarView)
The calendar position as one value: \fB.level(v.level)\fR with \fBv.date\fR as the view.
.TP
.B \&.min_level(l: Level)
The finest level (default \fBMonth\fR). Picking a cell at this level selects it (\fBon_pick\fR gets the first day of that month or year) instead of drilling down, so \fBmin_level(Level::Year)\fR is a month picker and \fBmin_level(Level::Decade)\fR a year picker.
.TP
.B \&.max_level(l: Level)
The coarsest level the title can zoom out to (default \fBDecade\fR); at it the title is dimmed and inert.
.TP
.B \&.today(d: Date)
The date "Today" jumps to and the current day, month and year are marked for (default: the system clock, UTC).
